<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sparda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 546–<a href="/years/334-bce">334 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Satrapy of Lydia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sparda was the Achaemenid Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, created after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a> around 546 BCE, with its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. Sardis became the main Persian capital in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the western end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. The satrapy lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> took Sardis in 334 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sparda.jpg" alt="Sparda" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mossmaps · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satrapy_of_Lydia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
